From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 15:37:29 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] zink: do not wait forever for another context to flush

A read map that finds the resource's last write recorded on a batch state
belonging to a different context waits for that batch to be submitted, and
`zink_batch_usage_unflushed_wait()` does that with an unbounded `cnd_wait()`.
The wait is only ever satisfied by whichever thread drives the other context.
That holds for an application with one thread per context; it does not hold for
a single-threaded multi-context user, and virglrenderer is one: it serves every
guest GL context, and its own ctx0 transfer context, from the thread that is
executing the map. No other thread exists to submit the batch, so the wait
never ends.

On this host that deadlock is fatal to the whole guest, not just to the frame.
The emulator's scanout CPU fallback reads the scanout resource through
`virgl_renderer_transfer_read_iov(res, ctx_id = 0)`, which runs `glReadPixels`
on ctx0 while the frame was rendered by a guest context. `zink_image_map()`
reaches the "force multi-context sync" wait, the vhost-user GPU thread parks in
`cnd_wait()` forever, the guest vCPU that kicked that virtqueue parks in
`vhost_user_recv_ack()` with local interrupts masked, and that CPU never takes
another timer tick: the guest reports an RCU stall every 63 seconds until it is
killed. Five such wedges are recorded in the OpenGL/GLES conformance campaigns
of 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-15, each one costing the run it was in.

Bound the wait. A flush that is on its way lands in microseconds, so ten
seconds without one means no thread is coming; report it and continue. Every
expiry is reported, because the read that follows misses that context's last
write.
Giving up returns false, the same answer the existing `trywait` path returns,
so the caller skips only the batch wait it asked for. Re-check the flag around
`cnd_timedwait()` as well: the previous single `cnd_wait()` also returned on a
spurious wakeup.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_batch.c | 46 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_batch.c b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_batch.c
index 846ae0a748f5981974093ae599f44e87d4cfe06a..81e9a063ae3e0922ad2f7a898980997d8c6dd788 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_batch.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_batch.c
@@ -8,6 +8,8 @@
 #include "zink_screen.h"
 #include "zink_surface.h"
 
+#include "util/timespec.h"
+
 #ifdef VK_USE_PLATFORM_METAL_EXT
 #include "QuartzCore/CAMetalLayer.h"
 #endif
@@ -1213,6 +1215,33 @@ zink_batch_usage_check_completion(struct zink_context *ctx, const struct zink_ba
    return zink_check_batch_completion(ctx, u->usage);
 }
 
+/* A batch that a *different* context left unflushed is submitted by whoever
+ * drives that context, and the wait below assumes that is another thread.  A
+ * single-threaded multi-context user -- a virtio-gpu renderer that serves every
+ * guest context, plus its own transfer context, from one thread -- has no other
+ * thread to do it, so the wait can never be satisfied and it takes the calling
+ * thread with it.  Put a generous deadline on the wait instead: a flush that is
+ * on its way lands in microseconds, so an expiry means none is coming.  Giving
+ * up costs the caller the ordering it asked for; blocking costs the process.
+ * Every expiry is reported, because the caller then reads the resource without
+ * that context's last write.
+ */
+#define ZINK_UNFLUSHED_WAIT_NS (10ull * 1000 * 1000 * 1000)
+
+static void
+unflushed_wait_expired(const struct zink_batch_usage *u)
+{
+   /* An unflushed batch has no id yet -- submit_queue assigns it -- so name
+    * the batch by the submission it is waiting to become.
+    */
+   mesa_logw("zink: submission %u of another context stayed unflushed for %u "
+             "seconds; continuing without it, so this read misses its writes "
+             "(a single-threaded multi-context caller cannot flush it from "
+             "here)",
+             u->submit_count + 1u,
+             (unsigned)(ZINK_UNFLUSHED_WAIT_NS / 1000000000ull));
+}
+
 bool
 zink_batch_usage_unflushed_wait(struct zink_context *ctx, struct zink_batch_usage *u, unsigned submit_count, bool trywait)
 {
@@ -1231,8 +1260,21 @@ zink_batch_usage_unflushed_wait(struct zink_context *ctx, struct zink_batch_usag
          if (trywait) {
             struct timespec ts = {0, 10000};
             cnd_timedwait(&u->flush, &u->mtx, &ts);
-         } else if (u->unflushed)
-            cnd_wait(&u->flush, &u->mtx);
+         } else {
+            struct timespec now, deadline;
+
+            timespec_get(&now, TIME_UTC);
+            /* Ten seconds from now cannot overflow a 64-bit time_t. */
+            ASSERTED bool overflow =
+               timespec_add_nsec(&deadline, &now, ZINK_UNFLUSHED_WAIT_NS);
+            assert(!overflow);
+            while (zink_batch_usage_is_unflushed(u)) {
+               if (cnd_timedwait(&u->flush, &u->mtx, &deadline) != thrd_success) {
+                  unflushed_wait_expired(u);
+                  break;
+               }
+            }
+         }
          mtx_unlock(&u->mtx);
       }
    }
